      { "text": "Blocking NCC prevents distal recapture of the sodium load escaping the thick ascending limb", "explanation": "Correct. When a loop diuretic blocks NKCC2, a large sodium load is delivered downstream and a substantial fraction is reclaimed at distal sites, especially by the NCC in the distal convoluted tubule. Adding a thiazide blocks that second reabsorptive site, so the escaped sodium continues down the nephron and is excreted. This sequential nephron blockade produces a synergistic natriuretic and diuretic effect and is a standard strategy in refractory edema." },
      { "text": "The combination restores the medullary osmotic gradient that the loop diuretic had abolished", "explanation": "Incorrect. Thiazides act distal to the thick ascending limb and play no part in generating the medullary gradient, so they cannot restore it. This would be the correct answer if a drug existed that rebuilt medullary tonicity during loop diuretic therapy." }
