      { "text": "It competitively antagonizes aldosterone at a cell surface G protein-coupled receptor on the luminal membrane", "explanation": "Incorrect. The mineralocorticoid receptor is an intracellular nuclear receptor in the cytosol, not a surface G protein-coupled receptor, which is precisely why the onset and offset are slow. This would be the correct answer if aldosterone signaled through a membrane receptor with second messengers." },
      { "text": "It binds a cytosolic receptor and blocks transcription of ENaC and ROMK", "explanation": "Correct. MR antagonists diffuse across the basolateral membrane and bind the mineralocorticoid receptor in the cytosol of principal cells. The drug-receptor complex translocates to the nucleus and binds response elements on DNA, blocking transcription of the genes aldosterone normally upregulates, including ENaC, the Na+/K+ ATPase and ROMK. Because the effect requires changes in gene expression and protein turnover, onset and offset lag well behind the plasma concentration, which is why eplerenone acts for 7-9 hours despite a half-life under 2 hours." },
      { "text": "It irreversibly alkylates the epithelial sodium channel, so recovery requires synthesis of new channels", "explanation": "Incorrect. No agent in this lecture alkylates ENaC; amiloride binds reversibly, and its long duration comes from high potency and slow dissociation rather than covalent modification. This would be the correct answer if a drug covalently inactivated its target, as aspirin does to cyclooxygenase." },
      { "text": "It inhibits 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, allowing cortisol to activate the receptor", "explanation": "Incorrect. That enzyme protects the mineralocorticoid receptor by converting cortisol to inactive cortisone; inhibiting it would cause apparent mineralocorticoid EXCESS with hypertension and hypokalemia, the opposite of a potassium-sparing effect. This would be the correct answer if the question concerned licorice ingestion." }
